#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:5
#: 1ceeba9e9ac142188a43b688a77ea763
msgid "Learning ArduPilot — Introduction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:7
#: 7971490d60ec450ba8b99905fb2c1cb0
msgid "This page introduces the basic structure of ArduPilot. Before you get started you should work out what code exploring system you will use. You could just use a web browser and look at https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/ but you will probably get a lot more out of it if you have :ref:`cloned all of the git repositories <where-to-get-the-code>` and use a good programmer's IDE like the ones recommended :ref:`here <code-editing-tools-and-ides>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:13
#: 9ff7bb47a7e747968ee24a9f5ca22040
msgid "Basic structure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:18
#: eee24dd2f67d4ca58eff0b7800871c07
msgid "The basic structure of ArduPilot is broken up into 5 main parts:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:20
#: 9f5371dec371457e87b7fa8ec222c6eb
msgid "vehicle code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:21
#: a47816d3a8194587b4071994de7ac5fb
msgid "shared libraries"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:22
#: 6b3df2b70c2d4bffb659e4613b6fcfae
msgid "hardware abstraction layer (AP_HAL)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:23
#: dbc2ef1557a04aff9775dc52e6b7689e
msgid "tools directories"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:24
#: c933e0462bf345a48af19b3aba9ba47f
msgid "external support code (e.g. mavlink)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:27
#: 061b6610d5df47589ce20c11676d7447
msgid "Vehicle Code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:29
#: 2b69cead29c04d67af62368757e58c92
msgid "The vehicle directories are the top level directories that define the firmware for each vehicle type.  Currently, there are 6 vehicle types: Plane, Copter, Rover, Sub, Blimp and AntennaTracker. Although there are a lot of common elements between different vehicle types, they are each different. For now, we only have a :ref:`detailed description of the code structure for the Copter code <apmcopter-code-overview>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:33
#: 449a153d53304d5cb8da08d58efdd930
msgid "Along with the \\*.cpp files, each vehicle directory contains a wscript file which lists library dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:37
#: 845c9e99a6da478e974a4b4ba43810ab
msgid "Libraries"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:39
#: 58d1bfb28d074200ac3494f34c33a4cf
msgid "The `libraries <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/tree/master/libraries>`__ are shared amongst all vehicle types.  These libraries include sensor drivers, attitude and position estimation (aka :ref:`EKF <ekf>`) and control code (i.e. PID controllers). See the :ref:`Library Description <apmcopter-programming-libraries>`, :ref:`Library Example Sketches <learning-ardupilot-the-example-sketches>` and :ref:`Sensor Drivers <code-overview-sensor-drivers>` pages for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:44
#: 6153aff6e0a2426fb6236fa288de69b6
msgid "AP_HAL"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:46
#: 3cffd4ab4c344436ad432b5851ecf951
msgid "The AP_HAL layer (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is how we make ArduPilot portable to lots of different platforms. There is a top-level AP_HAL in libraries/AP_HAL that defines the interface that the rest of the code has to specific board features, and then there is a AP_HAL_XXX subdirectory for each board type, for example, AP_HAL_ChibiOS for stm32-based boards, AP_HAL_ESP32 for ESP32 boards and AP_HAL_Linux for Linux based boards."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:55
#: f65a157463ab4311aeaf80558f57863a
msgid "Tools directories"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:57
#: 1351fc2df9e546c5ad9b8497011f1862
msgid "The tools directories are miscellaneous support directories. For example, tools/autotest provides the autotest infrastructure behind the `autotest.ardupilot.org <https://autotest.ardupilot.org/>`__ site and tools/Replay provides our log replay utility."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:63
#: 6a5215c02c534a7aaa2fab8b8a2b6509
msgid "External support code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:65
#: 331be63b7b4944069ff513001c850e25
msgid "On some platforms we need external support code to provide additional features or board support. Currently the external trees are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:68
#: f65cdaadc63443e798b4cb853d3a1dbb
msgid "`ChibiOS <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ChibiOS>`__ - the ChibiOS RTOS used on stm32-based boards"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:70
#: 626df322d59b4e96a8624c5c7067e2df
msgid "`DroneCAN <https://github.com/DroneCAN>`__ - the CANBUS implementation used in ArduPilot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:72
#: 81fc6b11fafc492ea676e555b85b09de
msgid "`mavlink <https://github.com/ArduPilot/mavlink>`__ - the mavlink protocol and code generator"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-introduction.rst:77
#: 615e3e7aabe54d28a6b8466f2a0d6c4e
msgid "Most of these are imported as :ref:`Git Submodules <git-submodules>` when you :ref:`build ArduPilot <building-the-code>`."
msgstr ""
